window.HB_SEARCH = [{"url":"adr/index.html","title":"Architecture decisions","section":"adr","text":"TODO: how to read an ADR, an amendment and an erratum. Record Title Status Date ADR-001 Platform blueprint — invariants, D26, D6, D22, D28 (system actors) ACCEPTED 2026-10-15 ADR-001-A1 Temporal server v1.29.7 / api v1.53.0, the D6-K startup guard and the D9 workload allow-list ACCEPTED 2026-10-20 ADR-001-A2 Erratum E2 and amendment A2 to ADR-001-A1, closing system-workflow and task-queue reach via service certificates (F2) ACCEPTED 2026-10-23 ADR-001-A5 Amendment A5 to ADR-001 I9 and CI-10, a cascade cycle suppresses the re-start (one start per tenant, origin, record and definition) instead of rejecting the write ACCEPTED 2026-10-31 ADR-001-A6 Amendment A6 to ADR-001 Annex A.2 and A.3 — the nava_erasure role, onboarding seeds the audit sealer, and start rows not tied to a record ACCEPTED 2026-11-02 ADR-001-E1 command inspection for continue-as-new ACCEPTED (inherited) 2026-10-15 ADR-001-E3 Erratum E3 to ADR-001 Annex A.3, nava_onboard has SELECT on its own row, not only its own provisioning row ACCEPTED 2026-10-25 ADR-002 GET /api/v1/me exposes the caller's group paths, tenant-scoped and with the tenant prefix stripped ACCEPTED 2026-10-27 ADR-002-E1 Erratum E1 to ADR-002, a line-scoped nolint pinned by a CI gate replaces the api.go forbidigo exclusion ACCEPTED 2026-10-28 ADR-003 A recorded interpreter history is retired only by raising GetVersion's minSupported (call kept), with an append-only tombstone CI can check ACCEPTED 2026-11-01 ADR-004 One audit log for records and configuration, sealed by a per-tenant hash chain and exportable (CR-AUDIT-1) ACCEPTED 2026-11-02 ADR-004-A1 Amendment A1 to ADR-004: only nava_audit_reader can read audit_log.diff and diff_salt, through the masking reader's own pool ACCEPTED 2026-11-04 ADR-004-E1 Erratum E1 to ADR-004, the R2 vocabulary and key shape for the two cascade audit entries, and diff_truncated in the R1 envelope ACCEPTED 2026-11-02 ADR-004-E2 Erratum E2 to ADR-004 R20 and audit-export-v1 §3/§4: only the trailer's verifiable must equal the computed value; header stubs is the request ACCEPTED 2026-11-02 ADR-004-E3 Erratum E3 to ADR-004 decision 2: the commit-check probe is planned on every call (force_custom_plan), Finding 5 tests the plan that runs, and the budget is rebased ACCEPTED 2026-11-03 ADR-004-E4 Erratum E4 to ADR-004-E3 (budget): in CI the absolute commit-check ceilings are report-only; the job fails only on a same-run ratio, on flatness, or when nothing was measured ACCEPTED 2026-11-03 ADR-004-E5 Erratum E5 to ADR-004: no referential actions on audited tables (group sync tombstones groups), and no secrets in record payloads ACCEPTED 2026-11-04 ADR-004-E6 Erratum E6 to ADR-004-E4 (accepted risk E4-R1): the uniform-regression cue is a per-model 1.2x band around the recorded baseline, not the absolute review threshold ACCEPTED 2026-11-05 ADR-004-E7 Erratum E7 to ADR-004 R16: A1 applies the 10 s interval when the tenant has unsealed entries; A2 stores the interval for later runs ACCEPTED 2026-11-07 ADR-004-E8 Erratum E8 to ADR-004-A1 A1-1 and A1-7: the reader also reads prev_hash, entry_hash and erasure_ref; the R20 head read is nava_app's lease statement ACCEPTED 2026-11-08 ADR-005 Record numbering — a number allocator outside the create transaction (L2s), declarative number formats linked to entity types, scoped counters ACCEPTED 2026-11-10"},{"url":"benchmarks/index.html","title":"Benchmarks","section":"benchmarks","text":"TODO: the performance budgets and how they are measured. Section Budget Owner Commit-check trigger ( audit_commit_check , ADR-004 decision 2), single subject Postgres \u0026 Data Modeling Specialist Commit-check trigger, import Postgres \u0026 Data Modeling Specialist Migration lock holds Migration A/B tenant-lock hold (S8 backfill, audit_backfill_s8() ): ACCESS EXCLUSIVE on tenants , entity_definition_versions and audit_log until COMMIT Postgres \u0026 Data Modeling Specialist Migration lock holds Migration B lock hold (P5-32): ACCESS EXCLUSIVE on tenants , entity_definitions , entity_definition_versions , audit_log , audit_chain_head and outbox until COMMIT (R27) Postgres \u0026 Data Modeling Specialist Record history Record history length ( GET /api/v1/records/{entity}/{id}/history ) Principal Software Engineer Record numbering Counter wait, gaps type (L2s) Postgres \u0026 Data Modeling Specialist Record numbering Create, 1 client, over HTTP Quality, Testing \u0026 CI Advisor Record numbering Create, 8 clients, gaps type Quality, Testing \u0026 CI Advisor Record numbering Waiver: create, 8 clients, gapless type (L1) Postgres \u0026 Data Modeling Specialist"},{"url":"database/index.html","title":"Database","section":"database","text":"TODO: tables by area, from the schema snapshot. Migrations Migration Purpose 00001_global_revokes.sql ADR-001 A.3 global settings. Runs as nava_migrator, the database owner. 00002_platform_tables.sql T09 (TICK-64): the platform tables of ADR-001 A.3, each under ENABLE + FORCE 00003_identity_tables.sql T16 (TICK-69): the identity tables group sync (T25) fills from Keycloak and 00004_tenant_email_domain.sql T18a (TICK-194): the email domains each tenant owns. InviteUser creates a 00005_purge_indexes.sql T13 follow-up (TICK-146): indexes for nava-purge's batch DELETEs 00006_pending_invite.sql T252a (TICK-254): the admins a tenant has invited who have not signed in 00007_email_domain_release.sql TICK-280 (follow-up from TICK-276 item 1, AR-036): nava_onboard may 00008_outbox_payload_references.sql T32c (TICK-214), CI-7 finding T32b-1 (docs/security/temporal-ci6.md): an 00009_outbox_payload_keys.sql T32f (TICK-240), CI-7 finding T32b-1 remainder (docs/security/temporal-ci6.md): 00010_principal_tombstone.sql TICK-220 (T25 follow-up): a principal who leaves the Keycloak directory is 00011_principal_tombstone_validate.sql TICK-220: validates 00010's principals_tombstone_check in its own 00012_drop_principals_delete_policy.sql TICK-265 (contract step of TICK-220): group sync tombstones a principal 20261028175712_idempotency_lease.sql T22a (TICK-212): a lease nava_app can extend, so a takeover of an abandoned 20261031150000_entity_definitions.sql P3-02 (TICK-340): entity types and their published, immutable versions 20261031160000_tenant_admin_group.sql P3-04 (TICK-342, PLAN-003 D-3-3): the platform id of each tenant's admins 20261101120000_tenant_admin_group_onboard_select.sql P3-04h (TICK-373, PLAN-003 D-3-3): \"active implies the tenant has an admin\" 20261101130000_entity_versions_published_by_check.sql P3-06c (TICK-383): every version after v1 names who published it, in the 20261101130100_entity_versions_published_by_validate.sql P3-06c (TICK-383): validates 20261101130000's 20261101180000_entity_definition_versions_jsonb_check.sql TICK-378: fixed_columns and states on entity_definition_versions must be 20261101180100_entity_definition_versions_jsonb_check_validate.sql TICK-378: validates the previous migration's fixed_columns and states 20261101190000_records_pipeline.sql P3-09 (TICK-353): records gains what the write pipeline needs, audit_log is 20261102100000_cascade_start.sql P3-12 (TICK-88): cascade_start, the start key of ADR-001 amendment A5 20261102120000_idempotency_response_headers.sql TICK-412: a replayed idempotent response restores an allow-listed set of 20261102120100_idempotency_response_headers_validate.sql TICK-412: validates the previous migration's response_headers CHECK in its 20261102130000_audit_log_v0.sql P5-10a (TICK-427), PLAN-005 D-5-1: S9's interim audit table becomes 20261102130100_tenant_auditor_group.sql P5-18 (TICK-434, PLAN-005 D-5-4): the platform id of each tenant's auditors 20261102233600_audit_log.sql P5-10b (TICK-450): ADR-004's audit_log, its chain head and the pipeline's 20261103100000_audit_commit_check.sql P5-11 (TICK-428): ADR-004 decision 2's commit check, as a function any 20261103120000_audit_backfill_s8.sql P5-15 (TICK-433): migration A's backfill (ADR-004 R25 A half, R26). Every 20261103150000_audit_commit_check_custom_plan.sql P5-11-E3 (TICK-508): audit_commit_check() is planned on every call 20261104013400_groups_tombstone.sql ADR-004-E5 step (i), expand (TICK-534; D1, D2, D5, D10, D11, I1, I5). Group 20261104013500_groups_tombstone_validate.sql ADR-004-E5 step (i) (TICK-534, I5): validates 20261104013400's 20261104043000_groups_contract.sql ADR-004-E5 step (ii-a), contract (TICK-538; D2, D10). Runs only after step 20261104043100_groups_contract_validate.sql ADR-004-E5 step (ii-b) (TICK-538; D10, CI-E5-8): validates 20261104043000's 20261104100000_drop_audit_log_v0.sql P5-17 (TICK-435): drops S9's audit_log_v0 (PLAN-005 D-5-1, moved here from 20261104110000_records_audit_commit_check.sql P5-13c (TICK-530): attach ADR-004 decision 2's commit check to records, 20261104133000_audit_reader_policies.sql ADR-004-A1 A1-3 (TICK-558): nava_audit_reader, the masking reader's own 20261104191200_auditor_group_active_tenant.sql P5-23 (TICK-568, ADR-004 with ADR-001-A6, PLAN-005 D-5-4): nava_onboard may 20261105100000_onboard_seal_start.sql P5-33 (TICK-441, ADR-001-A6.2, ADR-004 R16): onboarding seeds a tenant's 20261107053707_audit_migration_b.sql P5-32 (TICK-442): migration B (ADR-004 R25 B half, R26, R27; R16 20261107100000_audit_chain_head_dead_lineage.sql P5-34f (TICK-590, ADR-004 R17): the dead-lineage gauge. nava-purge, as 20261108120000_record_write_keys_actor.sql TICK-626 (AR-TICK-480-01, carried onto D-5-3): an idempotency key belongs 20261108120100_record_write_keys_actor_key.sql TICK-626 (AR-TICK-480-01): record_write_keys is unique per principal. 20261108124249_acl_tables.sql P3-30 (TICK-615): the S10 authorization tables (PLAN-003 D-3-2, the S10 20261108184658_record_shares_typed_principals.sql P3-30a (TICK-625, closes AR-S10-01): a record share names a principal of 20261109100000_acl_versions_shape_check.sql TICK-629 (deferred from P3-31): acl_versions.rules and masks must have 20261109100100_acl_versions_shape_check_validate.sql TICK-629: validates the previous migration's rules and masks CHECKs in 20261109120000_acl_versions_audit_commit_check.sql P3-35 (TICK-620): attach the audit commit check to acl_versions (ADR-004 20261109130000_audit_log_subject_acl_policy.sql P3-35 (TICK-620), contract amendment A3: registers the audit subject type 20261109130100_audit_log_subject_acl_policy_validate.sql P3-35 (TICK-620): validates the previous migration's audit_log subject_type 20261109140000_records_list_updated.sql TICK-640 (P3-34b): the default sort (-updated_at) and sort=updated_at. A 20261109140100_records_list_created.sql TICK-640 (P3-34b): sort=created_at, read forward (descending) or backward 20261109140200_records_list_state.sql TICK-640 (P3-34b): sort=state, ordered state, updated_at, id; and 20261109140300_records_list_group.sql TICK-640 (P3-34b): the access path for assignment_group_id: 20261109140400_records_list_requester.sql TICK-640 (P3-34b): the access path for requester_id: filter[requester]=\u003cp\u003e 20261109140500_records_list_assignee.sql TICK-640 (P3-34b): the access path for assignee_id: filter[assignee]=\u003cp\u003e and 20261109140600_drop_records_s9_list_indexes.sql TICK-640 (P3-34b): drops P3-09's list indexes, which the records_list_* 20261109140700_platform_database_jit_off.sql TICK-640 (P3-34b): JIT off for every session on the platform database. 20261110040000_entity_definitions_numbering_mode.sql P3-36f (TICK-659): each entity type chooses how its records are numbered 20261110040100_entity_definitions_numbering_mode_validate.sql P3-36f (TICK-659): validates entity_definitions_numbering_mode_check, added 20261110100000_records_list_group_state.sql TICK-656 (P3-36d): the group access path with state as an index condition."},{"url":"database/schema.html","title":"Schema snapshot (schema.json)","section":"database","text":"Schema snapshot: schema.json docs/handbook/src/database/schema.json describes the platform database schema as a freshly migrated database has it. The handbook build ( docs/handbook/build/build.py ) reads this file. It never parses the migration SQL and never connects to a database (TICK-718 D-HB-9, TICK-733). This page is the contract between the writer (TICK-733) and the handbook reader (TICK-719), as the Technical Lead decided on TICK-733. Regenerating it The snapshot is generated, never hand-edited. Regenerate it after a migration lands on main , and commit it with the handbook update for that change. Start the local stack ( docker compose up ), so that the migrate one-shot has migrated the platform database nava . The migrator also builds the runtime audit_log policies from db/audited-tables.yaml . From the repository root, run the one command: docker compose exec -T postgres sh -c 'PGPASSWORD=\"$(cat /run/pg_superuser_password)\" psql -h /var/run/postgresql -U pgadmin -d nava -XAtq -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1' \u003c docs/handbook/build/schema.sql | python3 docs/handbook/build/schema_json.py docs/handbook/src/database/schema.json On Windows, use python instead of python3 if that is your interpreter's name. The two build files do the following: docs/handbook/build/schema.sql reads the catalogue and prints one JSON document. Every name is sorted with COLLATE \"C\" (byte order), so the output does not depend on the database's default collation. docs/handbook/build/schema_json.py uses only the Python standard library. It checks the document against the key table below: exact keys per object, kind values, the partition-key rules, and byte order. It refuses a table outside schema public or a foreign table, and it refuses connection details (see the last section). It writes the file only when every check passes. Version 1 The file is UTF-8 with LF line endings, a two-space indent and one trailing newline. Non-ASCII characters are written as-is, not escaped. Every key below is always present: a string that does not apply is \"\" , and a list that is empty is [] . A reader ignores keys it does not know, so a new key is additive and does not change version . Renaming or removing a key bumps version . The snapshot covers schema public only. Tables are named by their bare name. goose_db_version , the migrator's bookkeeping table, is left out. Key Value version 1 . A reader rejects any other value. tables Every table in public , sorted by name . tables[].name The table name. tables[].kind Exactly one of table , partitioned or partition . tables[].partition_of A partition's parent table, else \"\" . tables[].partition_by A partitioned table's key ( pg_get_partkeydef ), for example RANGE (occurred_at) , else \"\" . tables[].partition_bound A partition's bound ( pg_get_expr(relpartbound) ), for example DEFAULT , else \"\" . tables[].rls Row level security flags. tables[].rls.enabled ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY. tables[].rls.forced FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY. tables[].columns Columns in ordinal ( attnum ) order. tables[].columns[].name Column name. tables[].columns[].type format_type , for example timestamp with time zone . tables[].columns[].nullable true unless the column is NOT NULL. tables[].columns[].default The deparsed default expression, else \"\" . tables[].primary_key Primary key columns in key order, [] when there is no primary key. tables[].foreign_keys Foreign keys sorted by name . A partition lists its own copies of its parent's. tables[].foreign_keys[].name Constraint name. tables[].foreign_keys[].columns Referencing columns in key order. tables[].foreign_keys[].ref_table Referenced table. tables[].foreign_keys[].ref_columns Referenced columns in key order. tables[].foreign_keys[].on_delete NO ACTION , RESTRICT , CASCADE , SET NULL or SET DEFAULT . tables[].indexes Indexes sorted by name , including the primary key's. A partition lists its own copies of its parent's. tables[].indexes[].name Index name. tables[].indexes[].method Access method, for example btree or gin . tables[].indexes[].unique true for a unique index. tables[].indexes[].columns Key columns in order. An expression index's entry is the deparsed expression. tables[].indexes[].include The INCLUDE columns, [] when there are none. tables[].indexes[].predicate A partial index's deparsed WHERE , else \"\" . tables[].policies pg_policies rows sorted by name . tables[].policies[].name Policy name. tables[].policies[].command SELECT , INSERT , UPDATE , DELETE or ALL . tables[].policies[].roles Roles, sorted. tables[].policies[].permissive true for PERMISSIVE, false for RESTRICTIVE. A RESTRICTIVE policy changes what isolation means, so the sensitivity check must see it. tables[].policies[].using The deparsed USING , else \"\" . tables[].policies[].check The deparsed WITH CHECK , else \"\" . The predicates are PostgreSQL's own deparse, so the tenant predicate reads exactly as catalog.TenantPred(\"tenant_id\") . Not in version 1: check and unique constraints, sequences, functions, triggers, grants, owners, comments and table areas. Grants come from db/grants.yaml , audited or exempt status from db/audited-tables.yaml , and the table-to-area mapping from docs/handbook/src/database/areas.yaml on the handbook side. What it never contains The file holds no DSN, host, port, role password, pg_hba.conf address or local path. Version 1 has no key that could carry one. schema_json.py refuses to write a file that matches any of its leak patterns, and HB-12's manual sensitivity sweep covers the file. A future version that adds a free-text key, such as comments or owners, needs a new sensitivity review first."},{"url":"glossary/index.html","title":"Glossary","section":"glossary","text":"TODO: terms used across the platform."},{"url":"index.html","title":"Nava handbook","section":"home","text":"Orientation for engineers: what the platform is, how it is built, and where each part lives. Every page describes only what is on main ; anything on a story branch is marked \"in progress\". TODO: sections overview and reading order (written by the content tasks of TICK-718). Vision System Architecture decisions Stories Workflows Database Security Benchmarks Operations Glossary"},{"url":"operations/index.html","title":"Operations","section":"operations","text":"TODO: the public API operations and how to run the stack. Audit operations Operation Method and path Summary Responses reseedAuditChain POST /api/v1/audit/chain:reseed Restart a dead audit seal lineage 200, 400, 401, 403, 404, 409, 429, 500, 501 listAuditEntries GET /api/v1/audit/entries The tenant's audit timeline 200, 400, 401, 403, 429, 500, 501 exportAuditEntries GET /api/v1/audit/entries:export Download a verifiable JSONL export of the audit log 200, 400, 401, 403, 409, 429, 500, 501"},{"url":"security/index.html","title":"Security","section":"security","text":"TODO: tenant isolation, authentication and the threat model."},{"url":"stories/index.html","title":"Stories","section":"stories","text":"TODO: one page per delivered story, from the story manifest."},{"url":"system/core-acl.html","title":"Core: record ACL (ParsePolicy, PolicyStore.Publish, CanRead/CanWrite, explain)","section":"system","text":"Purpose Each entity type has a versioned, allow-only access policy. It is checked in four steps: an admin's JSON is parsed and checked, the policy is published as a new acl_versions row, every read and write is evaluated against the newest version, and the explainer reports a decision with the same evaluator. The policy model and every rule are in the normative record-acl.md ; field masks are on core-masks . Entry point HTTP: getEntityTypeAccess , putEntityTypeAccess , explainAccess in internal/server/access.go (tenant admins only, authz.RequireTenantAdminTx first). Evaluation: authz.ACL ( internal/authz/aclstore.go ), the authz.Authorizer the pipeline and the record handlers hold. Steps # Step Where Invariant Proving test 1 Parse and check the PUT body: database shape rules first (so no raw constraint error), then unique rule ids and mask rules; collect every referenced id authz.ParsePolicy ( policycheck.go ) A stored policy always decodes TestParsePolicyAcceptsTheShapesTheCheckAccepts , TestParsePolicyRefusesWithPointers , TestParsePolicyCollectsEveryIdAsATenantRef 1b Mask fields must be top-level properties of the current schema; every id must be a live group or user of the transaction's tenant authz.CheckMaskFields , authz.NotInTenant ( explain.go ) I1: a policy names only its own tenant's ids; foreign and unknown ids answer alike TestCheckMaskFields 2 Publish version N+1 under the entity type's row lock, with one config / publish audit entry in the same transaction PolicyStore.Publish , publishLocked ( publish.go ); PolicyStore.PublishIfNone for the onboarding default audit_commit_check refuses an unaudited insert TestP335ACLPublishWithoutAnAuditEntryIsRefused 3 Load the newest policy of the type in the caller's transaction; refuse a principal of another tenant first PolicyStore.Current , PolicyStore.QueuePolicy , ACL.policy No published version fails closed TestPolicyStoreReadsTheNewestVersionOfItsTenant , TestNoPublishedACLRefusesUserWrites 3a Read one record: ACL.CanRead -\u003e Allows(pol, p, read, row) ; shares loaded pair-matched by SharesFor aclstore.go , acl.go Unreadable is the same 404 as missing TestACLCanRead , TestSharesMatchOnKindAndID 3b Write: ACL.CanWrite(before, after) ; both rows must be in write scope aclstore.go A write cannot move a record out of scope TestACLCanWrite , TestWriteScopeIsEnforcedBeforeAndAfter 3c List: CompileRead / ReadBranches render the same planned rules as SQL inside the query acl.go I5: ACL inside the query, Go and SQL agree TestCompileReadAgreesWithAllows , TestI5Agreement , TestI5SharedWithIsPairMatched 4 Explain: the target user's decision with the same Allows , and only the masked fields the asking admin may read authz.Explain , authz.PrincipalOf ( explain.go ) The explainer cannot disagree with enforcement TestExplainMatchesTheEnforcementOnState , TestExplainNeverListsAFieldTheAdminCannotRead , TestExplainWithNoPolicyDeniesAtVersionZero Errors Error HTTP not a tenant admin ( RequireTenantAdminTx ) 403 forbidden unknown or foreign entity type, no published version (GET), unknown record or user (explain) 404 not_found (one body) If-Match missing or * 428 if_match_required *authz.ConflictError (stale If-Match ) 412 version_conflict with the current ETag body not a JSON object 400 validation_failed ParsePolicy / CheckMaskFields / NotInTenant problems 422 invalid_access_policy with pointers authz.ErrNotVisible on a record read 404 not_found authz.ErrForbidden on a write 403 forbidden Notes The order of the PUT checks (admin, entity type, If-Match , body, write) and the default incident ACL are defined in the normative note. There is no separate \"compiler\" stage: CompileRead is the SQL rendering of the same planned rules Allows evaluates. Links Tables acl_versions , record_shares : database section . Masks: core-masks . Where the pipeline calls the ACL: core-write-pipeline . Isolation and ACL guarantees: security section ."},{"url":"system/core-cascades.html","title":"Core: cascade bounds (I9)","section":"system","text":"Purpose A record write can start a workflow, which can write a record, which can start another workflow. ADR-001 I9 bounds that chain two ways: every write carries a causation (origin id and depth) and a write deeper than the limit is refused, and a workflow definition starts at most once per (tenant, origin, record). This page shows where both checks run inside the write pipeline. Entry point Depth: Pipeline.perform in internal/records/pipeline/pipeline.go compares Command.Causation.Depth with config.CascadeDepthLimit ( internal/platform/config/config.go ) before any statement but the tenant read. Start key: cascade.ClaimStart(ctx, tx, cascade.Key) in internal/records/cascade/cascade.go , called by the event sink in the triggering write's transaction; a repeat is audited by pipeline.AuditSuppressed . Steps # Step Where Invariant Proving test 1 Refuse a given origin at depth 0 and a workflow write at depth 0; mint the origin for a depth-0 write Command.check , Pipeline.perform Origin and depth never come from a request or payload (A5-3, A5-7) TestDepthZeroWritesMintTheirOrigin , TestWorkflowWriteAtDepthZeroIsInvalidEntry 2 Refuse a write beyond the depth limit; write one cascade_too_deep audit entry in a transaction of its own on the TxRunner , committed before Write returns Pipeline.rejectTooDeep , appendTooDeep ( audit.go ) I9 depth bound; nothing in the caller's transaction changes TestCI10_DepthBeyondLimitRejectedAndAuditedOnTheRunner , TestCI10_TooDeepUpdateOfAnotherTenantsRecordLeaksNothing , TestTooDeepAuditFailure 3 Store the causation on the record row with the write write.causation The start key is read from the row, never passed in TestClaimStartReadsTheKeyFromTheRecord 4 Claim the start key K(tenant, origin, record, definition): lock the record row (naming the tenant), insert into cascade_start cascade.ClaimStart A5: at most one start per key; per tenant TestCI10_StartKeyIsPerTenant , TestCI10_ConcurrentClaimsOfOneKey , TestCI10_RetriedActivityCreatesNoSecondStart 5 Claimed: enqueue the start through outbox.EnqueueStart , which only takes a claimed cascade.Claim outbox producer I6: starts only through the outbox TestCascadeStartGuards 6 Repeat: enqueue nothing, append a cascade_cycle entry, let the write commit pipeline.AuditSuppressed A5-4: a cycle is suppressed, not failed TestCIA17CycleEntries , TestCI10_WriteBackToTriggeringRecordCommits , TestCI10_SiblingAndSelfRetriggeringWritesAreNotCycles Errors Error Meaning outbox.ErrCascadeTooDeep depth past config.CascadeDepthLimit ; joined with the audit failure if the entry could not be written cascade.ErrRecordNotFound no such record in the transaction's tenant, or no tenant cascade.ErrNoOrigin the record row has no causation cascade.ErrInvalidKey no record or definition in the key; AuditSuppressed called with a claimed start None of these reach an HTTP client: the record routes always write at depth 0. Notes The limit's value lives only in internal/platform/config/config.go ; this page does not copy it. Links The workflow side (outbox, relay, interpreter): workflows section . Tables cascade_start , outbox , audit_log : database section . The write this hangs off: core-write-pipeline ."},{"url":"system/core-masks.html","title":"Core: field masks on read and write","section":"system","text":"Purpose A field mask hides one top-level property from every principal its read subject does not name, and makes it unwritable for every principal its write subject does not name. Masks live in the acl_versions document of the entity type, in its masks array beside rules , and are published, versioned and audited with the rules ( core-acl ). The rules are in the normative record-field-masks.md . Entry point Write: write.canWriteFields in internal/records/pipeline/pipeline.go calls ACL.CanWriteFields ( internal/authz/aclstore.go ), which calls authz.CheckWriteFields ( internal/authz/mask.go ). Read: the record handlers call authz.Shape through shapeRecord ( internal/server/records.go ); lists call authz.CheckQueryFields ( internal/server/records_list.go ); the audit timeline gets the caller's masks from ACL.ReadMasks . Steps # Step Where Invariant Proving test 1 Decide per field and principal: Readable / Writable ; only Groups and Users in a mask subject match; every mask on a field must admit authz.Readable , authz.Writable ( mask.go ) A2: one answer per caller and field, row-independent TestReadableAndWritableFollowGroupsAndUsers , TestRowDependentMaskSubjectsNeverMatch , TestEveryMaskOnAFieldMustAdmit 2 Write check, first on the caller's data (before defaults, validation, state, CanWrite , unique); system sources skip it write.canWriteFields , ACL.CanWriteFields , authz.CheckWriteFields , authz.WrittenFields I5: masks apply on write; no other error depends on a masked field TestFieldCheckRunsBeforeValidationAndTheStateModel , TestFieldCheckSeesOnlyTheCallersWrite , TestFieldNotWritableWritesNothing 2a A patch key the caller cannot read counts as a write whatever its value authz.CheckWriteFields A patch is no oracle for a masked value TestUnreadablePatchKeyIsAlwaysAWrite , TestPatchingAMaskedFieldIsRefusedEvenWithItsStoredValue 3 Shape every record body and every history before / after authz.Shape , authz.MaskedFields I5: masks apply on read; input never mutated TestShapeDropsMaskedFieldsAndNeverMutates 4 Refuse a list filter or sort on a field the caller cannot read, before any SQL authz.CheckQueryFields A hidden field cannot be filtered, sorted or counted TestCheckQueryFields 5 Redact masked fields in audit-timeline diffs ACL.ReadMasks -\u003e read.FieldMasks The reader role needs no new grant see internal/audit/read tests Errors Error HTTP *authz.ErrFieldNotWritable{Fields} 422 field_not_writable , one pointer per field, rule mask *authz.ErrMaskedField{Field} 400 masked_field a mask naming requester , assignee or shared , or an unknown property 422 invalid_access_policy at publish Links Storage ( acl_versions.masks ): database section . Masking guarantees: security section ."},{"url":"system/core-metadata.html","title":"Core: metadata engine (entity definitions and versions)","section":"system","text":"Purpose A tenant describes each entity type (incident, request, change, or its own) as a JSON Schema 2020-12 document plus a number prefix, fixed-column mapping and state model. The metadata engine turns that definition into a *metadata.Compiled , the one object every record write validates against, and decides whether a new version can be published over the current one. This page shows where each step lives; the rules themselves are in the normative metadata-engine.md . Entry point HTTP: createEntityType and publishEntityTypeVersion in internal/server/entitytypes.go . Go: entitytypes.Store.Create and entitytypes.Store.Publish ( internal/entitytypes/entitytypes.go ), which call metadata.Compile and metadata.Classify . Read side, used by the record pipeline: entitytypes.Store.Current and entitytypes.Store.Version (the pipeline.DefinitionReader interface), and entitytypes.Store.QueueCurrent for the batched prefetch. internal/metadata is pure: no database, network or file-system import. internal/entitytypes is the database half. Steps # Step Where Invariant Proving test 1 Refuse a schema that marks a property secret and also requires it, or carries a secret-looking default entitytypes.CheckSchemaSecrets ( audit.go ), entitytypes.CheckSchemaSecretRequired ( secretrequired.go ) ADR-004-E5 D9 TestCheckSchemaSecretRequired , TestAdversarialSecretDefaultIsRefused 2 Scan size and depth, walk the schema (local $ref only, closed root), compile with a fresh jsonschema compiler and a deny-all loader metadata.Compile ( compile.go ), denyAllLoader , walker.walk , walker.checkRefs One compiler per call, so no tenant sees another's schema TestCompileRefusesExternalRefs , TestCompilerLoaderDeniesEverything , TestSharedCompilerWouldLeak , TestCompileCapsAreInclusive 3 Check defaults against their own schema, fixed-column types and the state enum metadata.Compile A definition the pipeline cannot satisfy never stores TestCompileRefusesBadStateModelsAndShapes , TestCompileAcceptsBaseDefinition 4 Create: key, then prefix uniqueness; insert type and version 1 with compat = 'initial' ; append the config audit entry entitytypes.Store.Create , auditVersion Every config change is audited in the same transaction TestCreateRefusals 5 Publish: lock the type row, compile the next version, classify it against the current one entitytypes.Store.Publish , metadata.Classify ( classify.go ) Fail-closed classification: anything not listed is Breaking TestClassify , TestClassifyRemoveTransition , TestPublishRefusalsAndFailures 6 Insert version N+1 (number from max(version) + 1 under the row lock), move current_version , audit entitytypes.Store.Publish Concurrent publishes queue and number with no gap or duplicate TestPublishRefusalsAndFailures 7 Change numbering mode ( gaps or gapless ) with its own audited config entry entitytypes.Store.SetNumberingMode See core-numbering TestP336fSetNumberingMode , TestP336fNumberingModeCrossTenant 8 Use at write time: defaults then validation against the current version; stored rows are read under the version they were written with metadata.Compiled.ApplyDefaults , metadata.Compiled.Validate ( validate.go ), called from pipeline.write.create / write.update I4: JSON Schema runs before every write TestApplyDefaults , TestValidateReportsEachKindOfFailure , TestUpdateUnderNewVersionFillsDefaults The tenant on every statement comes from the caller's tenancy.InTenantTx transaction (row-level security plus the explicit tenant_id the inserts take). See core-tenancy . Errors Error Where HTTP *metadata.ErrInvalidDefinition (sorted JSON pointers) metadata.Compile 422 invalid_definition entitytypes.ErrKeyExists Store.Create 409 entity_type_exists entitytypes.ErrPrefixTaken Store.Create 409 number_prefix_taken *entitytypes.BreakingError{Paths} Store.Publish 422 schema_breaking_change *entitytypes.SecretRequiredError CheckSchemaSecretRequired 422 definition_secret_required *entitytypes.SecretInContentError CheckSchemaSecrets 422 config_secret_in_content entitytypes.ErrNotFound Store.Publish , Store.GetVersion 404 not_found (another tenant's type is the same 404) The mapping is in internal/server/entitytypes.go ; codes and statuses are in api/errors.yaml . Notes Seed definitions incident , request and change live in internal/metadata/templates/ ( metadata.Template ) and are seeded at onboarding by entitytypes.Store.Seed . The compatibility classes and the caps are listed only in the normative note; this page does not repeat them. Links Tables: database section ( entity_definitions , entity_definition_versions ). Next step of a write: core-write-pipeline . ADR-001 I4 and I8 (configuration, not code): ADR summaries ."},{"url":"system/core-numbering.html","title":"Core: record numbering (gaps and gapless)","section":"system","text":"Purpose Every record gets a human number, \u003cprefix\u003e\u003c7 digits\u003e ( INC0000001 ), unique and increasing per tenant and entity type. Numbers come from one record_counters upsert; there is no sequence (ADR-001 I8). A type's numbering_mode picks where the upsert runs: gaps (default, ADR-005 L2s): in a transaction of its own on the allocator pool, committed before the record is inserted. The counter row is locked for one round trip. A create that fails after its number leaves a **gap by design**. gapless (ADR-005 L1): last in the create's own write batch, so a rollback gives the number back, at the cost of holding the counter lock until the create commits. The decision and its rules are in the normative ADR-005 and the numbering section of record-write-pipeline.md . Entry point write.create in internal/records/pipeline/pipeline.go reads Compiled.NumberingMode() and calls write.allocate ( alloc.go ) for a gaps type. PoolRunner.Allocate -\u003e tenancy.AllocTx -\u003e store.QueueAllocate ( internal/records/store/numbering.go ). gapless : store.QueueInsertNumbered in write.writeCreate ( batch.go ). Mode change: entitytypes.Store.SetNumberingMode . Pool wiring: cmd/nava-core/allocator.go ( NAVA_ALLOCATOR_DATABASE_URL ). Steps # Step Where Invariant Proving test 1 Take the number only after the replay check, validation, the write check and the unique and group checks write.create I4: a refused, invalid or replayed create never moves the counter TestRefusedCreatesLeaveTheCounter , TestCreateReplayTakesNoNumber 2 gaps : allocate in its own transaction on the allocator pool; tenant from the create's transaction, never a parameter write.allocate , PoolRunner.Allocate , tenancy.AllocTx , store.QueueAllocate ADR-005 A1, A2, A4 TestCreateTakesItsNumberInATransactionOfItsOwn , TestAllocationTransactionIsTenantScoped 3 Check the allocation names this create's tenant, type, scope '' and epoch 0; store its now() as created_at write.allocate ADR-005 A2, A7 TestCreateChecksTheAllocation , TestAllocationIsTheCreatesAndDatesTheRecord 4 Give up past the allocation timeout with ErrRetryable ; record acquire and total time PoolRunner.Allocate , AllocMetrics ADR-005 A5, A8 TestAllocationTimeoutIsRetryableAndMeasured 5 The allocator pool is separate from the API pool and logs in as nava_app only tenancy.AllocPoolConfig , tenancy.CheckAllocatorRole ADR-005 A3; I3 TestAllocatorPoolDoesNotStarveThePool , TestAllocatorPoolRefusesABypassRLSRole 6 A failure after the allocation commits leaves a gap; numbers are never reused gaps_integration_test.go Gaps by design TestRolledBackCreateLeavesAGap , TestCreateRollsBackAtEveryStage 7 gapless : upsert last in the write batch; rollback returns the number store.QueueInsertNumbered ADR-005 L1 TestRolledBackCreateNumberIsReused , TestConcurrentCreatesNumberWithoutGapsThroughPgBouncer 8 A mode change applies to creates that start after it; both modes upsert the same row entitytypes.Store.SetNumberingMode No number used twice TestModeSwitchNeverReusesANumber , TestNumberingModesDoNotInterfere 9 Format \u003cprefix\u003e\u003c7 digits\u003e , growing past 7; lists sort on created_at store.FormatNumber Unique per tenant and type TestFormatNumber , TestNumbersPerTenantAndType Errors Error HTTP ErrRetryable (allocation past its timeout) 503 retry_later , nothing committed but possibly the number (a gap) allocation not this create's (tenant, type, scope, epoch) 500 internal_error (a bug, never expected) entitytypes.ErrInvalidNumberingMode refused before anything is written Notes Metrics: nava_numbering_alloc_acquire_seconds , nava_numbering_alloc_seconds , nava_numbering_alloc_timeouts . Measured create latency under both modes: benchmarks page . Links Table record_counters : database section . The allocator transaction: core-tenancy . The surrounding write: core-write-pipeline ."},{"url":"system/core-tenancy.html","title":"Core: tenant transactions (InTenantTx, batching, allocator pool)","section":"system","text":"Purpose Every tenant-scoped database access in nava-core runs inside tenancy.InTenantTx . It is the only Go code that sets app.tenant_id , the setting every row-level security policy reads, and it sets it in the same round trip as BEGIN . This page walks the path from a verified token to a scoped transaction, and the helpers the record pipeline uses on top of it (first-batch BEGIN, commit batch, memo, allocator transaction, lock timeout). The rules are in the normative tenant-transactions.md , tenant-tx-batching.md and tenant-resolution.md . Entry point Resolution: internal/platform/auth middleware calls identity.IdentityResolver.Resolve ; handlers read the tenant with identity.FromContext . Never from a path, query, body or header. tenancy.InTenantTx(ctx, pool, tenantID, fn, opts...) in internal/tenancy/tenancy.go . tenancy.TenantOf(ctx, tx) : the tenant a transaction runs under, used by the pipeline, the ACL store and the cascade claim instead of a parameter. Steps # Step Where Invariant Proving test 1 Resolve the tenant from the validated token and the tenant binding; only an active tenant resolves internal/platform/auth , keycloak.Adapter.Resolve , pgstore.Binding I2: tenant only from a platform-verified source TestNoTenant (empty tenant), resolution tests listed in tenant-resolution.md 2 Open the transaction: BEGIN; SELECT set_config('app.tenant_id', \u003cuuid\u003e, true) in one simple-query round trip, transaction-local tenancy.InTenantTx , tenancy.BeginTenant I1: RLS sees the tenant before any statement of fn TestBeginSetsTheTenantInOneRoundTrip , TestTenantIsTransactionLocal , TestTenantNotVisibleThroughPgBouncer 3 With BeginInFirstBatch() , defer BEGIN into the first pgx.Batch (BEGIN and tenant first, then the caller's statements) lazyTx.SendBatch , lazyTx.begin ( firstbatch.go ), tenancy.FirstBatchTenant No statement runs before the tenant; a failure aborts everything TestFirstBatchRunsUnderTheTenant , TestFirstBatchCannotReachAnotherTenant , TestFirstBatchFailureKeepsNothing 4 Per-transaction memo for prefetched reads (definition, policy, the row a create stored); savepoint rollback undoes it tenancy.Remember , Recall , Forget ( scopedTx , memo ) The memo dies with the transaction, which has one tenant TestMemoIsPerTransaction , TestSavepointRollbackUndoesItsMemo 5 Queue statements for the round trip that commits tenancy.ExecAtCommit , tenancy.QueueAtCommit , scopedTx.commitBatch A failing queued statement skips COMMIT TestExecAtCommit , TestCreateRecordFailureInFirstOrLastTripLeavesNothing 6 Bound one statement's lock wait (the idempotency claim) without a round trip tenancy.QueueUnderLockTimeout ( locktimeout.go ) Only that statement waits at most the bound (55P03) TestQueueUnderLockTimeout , TestQueueUnderLockTimeoutFloor 7 Run a number allocation in its own transaction on the allocator pool, tenant from the create's transaction, one pipelined send, asynchronous commit tenancy.AllocTx , tenancy.AllocPoolConfig , tenancy.CheckAllocatorRole ( alloc.go ) I3: the pool logs in as nava_app only, never SUPERUSER or BYPASSRLS TestAllocTxIsOneSendAndLeavesNoSetting , TestAllocTxFailureLeaksNothing , TestAllocatorPoolRefusesOtherRoles 8 End: COMMIT on success; ROLLBACK on error (error returned unwrapped, a rollback failure joined) or panic (re-raised) tenancy.InTenantTx The setting ends with the transaction TestErrorRollsBack , TestFailedRollbackIsJoined , TestPanicRollsBackAndReraises , TestCommitFailureIsReturned Every request in the tenancy, pipeline and server tests runs through tenancytest.Guard , a pgx tracer that records any statement sent on a connection with no tenant. The cross-tenant path registry is internal/tenancy/isolation . Record-create round trips (TICK-692) A gaps record create is four round trips: Round trip What 1 BEGIN + tenant, idempotency claim under QueueUnderLockTimeout , prefetch (definition, ACL policy) 2 checks: unique locks and lookups, assignment group, payload preview 3 number: tenancy.AllocTx on the allocator pool (its own COMMIT, asynchronous) 4 INSERT records (asserted), write key, audit entry, idempotency completion, COMMIT The statement-level table and the history of the budget are in tenant-tx-batching.md ; measured latency against the budget is on the benchmarks page . Errors Error Meaning tenancy.ErrNoTenant uuid.Nil tenant, or TenantOf on a transaction with no tenant; no transaction is opened tenancy.ErrAllocatorRole the allocator pool would log in as another role or a privileged one; startup refuses SQLSTATE 55P03 a statement under QueueUnderLockTimeout waited past its bound; the HTTP layer answers 409 idempotency_in_flight for the claim Notes navalint setconfig reports set_config / app.tenant_id outside internal/tenancy ; navalint tenancyconn reports .Conn() and .LargeObjects() on a pgx.Tx outside it. The database grants are the control; the lint is defence in depth. The tracer exports no SQL text and no parameter values ( TestTracedTxExportsNoValues ). Links Policies, roles and grants: database section . Who uses it next: core-write-pipeline , core-numbering ."},{"url":"system/core-write-pipeline.html","title":"Core: record write pipeline (write, read, list)","section":"system","text":"Purpose pipeline.Write is the only way to create or update a record (ADR-001 I4). It runs inside the caller's tenancy.InTenantTx transaction and never commits, so the record, its write key, its audit entry and whatever the EventSink wrote commit together or not at all. Reads go through pipeline.Get and pipeline.List ; only the pipeline imports internal/records/store . The rules are in the normative record-write-pipeline.md . Entry point HTTP: createRecord ( handlers.CreateRecord , internal/server/records.go ), patchRecord ( internal/server/records_patch.go ), getRecord , listRecords ( internal/server/records_list.go ). Go: (*pipeline.Pipeline).Write(ctx, tx, pipeline.Command) in internal/records/pipeline/pipeline.go ; pipeline.Get , pipeline.QueueGet and pipeline.List in internal/records/pipeline/read.go . Other writers (workflows, rules) call the same Write with a system Source ; the architecture test TestRecordWritersOnlyInPipeline fails on any other writer of records . Steps # Step Where Invariant Proving test 1 Check the command's shape: op and record, source, a user write with an idempotency key, a depth-0 write that names an origin ( Write mints it), a workflow write at depth 0, an AI write without source_ref Command.check A cascade origin is minted, never given (ADR-001 A5) TestWriteRefusesBadCommands , TestDepthZeroWritesMintTheirOrigin 2 Tenant from the transaction; an actor of another tenant is refused Pipeline.perform , tenancy.TenantOf I1, I2 TestWriteTakesTheTenantFromTheTransaction , TestWriteRefusesAnActorOfAnotherTenant , TestCrossTenantWritesRefused 3 Prefetch in one batch (definition id, write key, current definition, ACL policy); on the HTTP create the same batch carries BEGIN and the request's idempotency claim Pipeline.QueuePrefetch , Pipeline.prefetch ( batch.go ), idempotency.ResolveClaim in the handler The claim is the transaction's first write TestCreateRecordFailureInFirstOrLastTripLeavesNothing 4 Replay a keyed system write from record_write_keys , per principal replay , store.QueueWriteKeyByKey I7: a retry never acts twice TestIdempotencyKeyReplays , TestIdempotencyKeyIsScopedByPrincipal , TestIdempotencyKeyMismatchConflicts 5 Update only: load the record, check read access (unreadable = not found), then the version write.update , store.Get , authz.SharesFor , ACL.CanRead No version or existence oracle TestUpdateOfAnUnreadableRecordIsNotFound , TestConcurrentUpdatesOneWins 6 Mask write check on the caller's own data, before anything else can depend on a masked field write.canWriteFields -\u003e ACL.CanWriteFields I5 (see core-masks ) TestFieldCheckRunsBeforeValidationAndTheStateModel , TestFieldNotWritableWritesNothing 7 JSON Merge Patch (update), defaults, JSON Schema, state model mergePatch ( patch.go ), Compiled.ApplyDefaults , Compiled.Validate , Compiled.AllowsTransition I4: validation before every write TestCreateValidatesBeforeWriting , TestInvalidCreateWritesNothing , TestIncidentTransitions 8 Split fixed columns out of data ; default requester_id to the caller on a user/AI create store.Record.Split , write.defaultRequester A fixed property is stored only in its column TestFixedColumnsOnlyInTheirColumn 9 Row write check on before and after rows (skipped for rule , workflow , function , system ) write.canWrite -\u003e ACL.CanWrite I5 (see core-acl ) TestWriteChecksTheRowBeforeAndAfter , TestOnlyTheSystemSourcesSkipTheAuthorizer 10 Unique properties: pg_advisory_xact_lock on the canonical value, then a SELECT ; assignment group must be live write.checkUnique , store.LockUnique , store.UniqueTaken , write.checkGroup , store.GroupLive ; batched on create by write.checkCreate Safe under PgBouncer transaction pooling TestUniqueConcurrentCreatesOneWins , TestUniqueConcurrentUpdateAndCreateOneWins , TestCreateChecksEveryUniquePropertyInOneBatch , TestGroupLive 11 Number (create): see core-numbering write.allocate I4: a refused create never moves the counter TestRefusedCreatesLeaveTheCounter 12 Write: create INSERT (queued at commit and asserted against the 201, or sent now); update UPDATE ... version = version + 1 WHERE version = expected write.writeCreateAtCommit , store.QueueInsertRecordAsExpected , write.writeCreate , store.Update One version bump per write (optimistic concurrency) TestInsertRecordAsExpected , TestEveryWriteBumpsVersionOnce , TestCreateChecksWhatTheInsertStored 13 Keyed write row, audit entry (diff built from the stored jsonb; secrets refused), event sink, all in the same transaction write.finish , store.InsertWriteKey , appendRecordEntry -\u003e audit.Append , EventSink.RecordChanged I6 entry point: the sink enqueues outbox rows in the write's transaction TestAuditAndSinkInTheWriteTransaction , TestDiffIsBuiltFromStoredJSONB , TestSecretInRecordDiffIsRefused , TestCreateRollsBackAtEveryStage , TestPipelineKill Read and list Get : pipeline.Get answers a record created in the same transaction from the memo, otherwise store.Get . The handler then calls ACL.CanRead (unreadable is the same 404 as missing) and shapes the body with authz.Shape . pipeline.QueueGet puts the read in the GET's single batch. List : the handler builds the predicate with authz.CompileRead (or authz.ReadBranches for one index-ordered branch per subject) and checks every filter and sort field with authz.CheckQueryFields , before any SQL. store.List ( list.go ) counts (capped at TotalCap ) and pages by keyset over a listable column; a data-property filter runs under DataFilterTimeout and maps a cancel to ErrStatementTimeout . Proving tests: TestCompileReadAgreesWithAllows , TestBranchedStatements , TestTimedOutMapsOnlyADataFilterCancel . Errors Generated from the mapping in handlers.recordError ( internal/server/records.go ) and the PATCH handler ( internal/server/records_patch.go ), statuses from api/errors.yaml . Every pipeline error raised after the definition was read is wrapped in *pipeline.ErrAtVersion , so error pointers are built from the version the write used. Pipeline result HTTP code ErrNotFound (also an unreadable record on update) 404 not_found authz.ErrForbidden 403 forbidden *authz.ErrFieldNotWritable 422 field_not_writable *ErrValidation (incl. rule audit_secret_in_diff ) 422 record_invalid *ErrIllegalTransition 422 illegal_transition ErrAssignmentGroupRemoved 422 assignment_group_removed *ErrUniqueViolation 409 unique_violation (names the property only) ErrVersionConflict 412 version_conflict (current ETag re-read) ErrRetryable (deadlock, serialization failure, allocation timeout) 503 retry_later foreign-key 23503 on a fixed column 422 record_invalid (rule reference ) ErrInvalidCommand , *ErrIdempotencyConflict , outbox.ErrCascadeTooDeep 500 internal_error : the HTTP handlers never send a key or causation, so these are system-writer results only The HTTP request's own Idempotency-Key is a separate layer ( idempotency_keys , 409 idempotency_key_reuse / idempotency_in_flight ), not record_write_keys . Notes The handbook generator has no error-table block (the TICK-720 gate was cancelled), so the table above is written by hand from the two files named; check it against them when either changes. The round-trip layout of a create is on core-tenancy . Links Tables: database section . What the event sink starts: workflows section ; cascade bounds on core-cascades ."},{"url":"system/index.html","title":"System","section":"system","text":"TODO: components and how a request flows through them. See also the viewer sample ."},{"url":"system/viewer-sample.html","title":"Viewer sample","section":"system","text":"The viewer gives every diagram zoom, pan, reset and full screen. Authors write nothing extra. Remove this page when real diagrams exist. Mermaid Excalidraw Search target The word flamingo appears only on this page, so search can be checked."},{"url":"vision/index.html","title":"Vision","section":"vision","text":"TODO: what the platform is for and who it serves."},{"url":"workflows/index.html","title":"Workflows","section":"workflows","text":"TODO: the durable workflows and their activities."}];
